  "overview": {
    "claims": [
      {
        "attribution": "Leslie Rutledge, Attorney General of Arkansas (Petitioner)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-699224459d84-0000-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 61,
              "start": 1
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Leslie Rutledge, Attorney General of Arkansas (Petitioner), according to Leslie Rutledge, Attorney General of Arkansas (Petitioner), pharmacy Benefit Managers (PBMs) act as intermediaries that reimburse pharmacists at rates often below their costs, which can force pharmacies to close and reduce access in rural Arkansas."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Bronni",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-699224459d84-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 61,
              "start": 1
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Bronni, the state requires PBMs to reallocate part of their margin back to local pharmacies so that independent rural pharmacies can remain in business and communities retain access to pharmacy services."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Leslie Rutledge, Attorney General of Arkansas (Petitioner)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-699224459d84-0000-0003",
              "ev-699224459d84-0001-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 61,
              "start": 1
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The petitioner argues that ERISA does not preempt state laws that implement or enforce rate regulation, and that Act\u202f900\u2019s enforcement mechanisms are permissible under Arkansas law."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Waxman",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-699224459d84-0001-0008"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 61,
              "start": 1
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Waxman, the respondent contends that Act\u202f900 directly compels ERISA plan administrators to follow state\u2011specific rules, creating a patchwork that makes uniform plan administration impossible."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Frederick Liu",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-699224459d84-0001-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 61,
              "start": 1
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Frederick Liu, amicus brief by the United States frames the central legal question as whether Arkansas law directly regulates a central matter of plan administration, which would be impermissible under ERISA."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "J.B. Hunt Company",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "699224459d84f531d4d1f9b20f538451ac65618843da57e2bd34ba0348f02e33",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-699224459d84-0002-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 61,
              "start": 1
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to J.B. Hunt Company, amicus brief by J.B. Hunt emphasizes the difficulty of achieving uniform plan administration across states."
      }
    ],
    "heading": "Overview",
    "status": "complete",
    "summary": null,
    "summary_citations": []
  },
